"The dot product is undefined for zero vectors.","Zero vectors have negative length."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1bd28cf54c5d700711d716d9"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $x\\cdot y \\gt 0$, what kind of angle do nonzero vectors $x$ and $y$ make?","options":["An obtuse angle.","An acute angle.","No angle can be defined.","A right angle."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9bee4aa2da9ab9d5ac836f1a"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $x\\cdot y=0$ for nonzero vectors, what is their angle?","options":["An obtuse angle.","A zero angle.","An acute angle.","A right angle."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8844006819fcd52c00236f99"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a negative dot product indicate about the angle between two nonzero vectors?","options":["The angle is obtuse.","The vectors must be equal.","The angle is acute.","One vector must be zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1f182901d7655789a544fb2c"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the angle between $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$?","options":["$\\pi/2$","$\\pi$","$\\pi/4$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"93f0c4866f5f91d9c2c7d321"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the angle between $(1,0)$ and $(1,0)$?","options":["$0$","$\\pi/4$","$\\pi$","$\\pi/2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"251220c6d06b6ec709b76d35"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the angle between $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$?","options":["$\\pi$","$0$","$\\pi/4$","$\\pi/2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c757252fca46296b1eb8dd83"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,0)$ and $y=(1,1)$, what is the sign of $x\\cdot y$?","options":["Positive.","Undefined.","Zero.","Negative."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ba23e54e1bf9133113a2a346"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,0)$ and $y=(0,-2)$, what is $x\\cdot y$?","options":["$0$","$1$","$-2$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7110c7b561a768cb60db2766"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How does Cauchy-Schwarz support the angle formula?","options":["It says the denominator is always zero.","It ensures the cosine ratio lies in $[-1,1]$.","It replaces cosine by determinant.","It makes every angle acute."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3bd2eb501580d38df41f88c0"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What information about direction is contained in the sign of $x\\cdot y$?","options":["Whether the vectors have integer entries, under all circumstances whatsoever.","The number of coordinates in each vector.","Whether the vectors point more similarly, perpendicularly, or oppositely.","The exact length of each vector."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cfb74005a5aa1a0634abe526"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does scaling one vector by a positive scalar not change the angle?","options":["The norm of the scaled vector becomes zero, for every possible choice of input.","The formula stops using the dot product.","The vector becomes orthogonal to itself.","The dot product and the norm product are scaled by the same positive factor."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e946f86d380fc1dda3e37ec2"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,0)$ and $y=(1,1)$, what is $\\cos\\theta$?","options":["$-1/\\sqrt2$","$0$","$1/\\sqrt2$","$\\sqrt2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e2c776b6c9a721534f749d2b"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,1)$ and $y=(1,-1)$, what is the angle between them?","options":["$\\pi/4$","$0$","$\\pi/2$","$\\pi$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c23d4413f66efad878759380"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $u$ and $v$ are unit vectors with $u\\cdot v=-1/2$, what is $\\cos\\theta$?","options":["$-2$","$-1/2$","$1/2$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"88e3cdc13d72e876c23809c7"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the angle between nonzero vectors usually taken in $[0,\\pi]$?","options":["The cosine function is positive only on that interval.","That interval gives a unique angle determined by the cosine value.","Angles outside the interval make dot products undefined.","Vectors cannot be opposite in $\\mathbb R^n$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b3ad20af8c38399aad38492f"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What goes wrong if one tries to define the angle between $0$ and a nonzero vector using the formula?","options":["The cosine value is automatically $1$.","The denominator contains $\\|0\\|=0$.","The numerator is too large.","The angle must be obtuse."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7fdf0f2004fb726d40a35945"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,2)$ and $y=(2,1)$, what is $\\cos\\theta$?","options":["$5/4$","$4/5$","$0$","$3/5$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"49e62882953ea53908751f5f"},{"id":"la_batch12_angle_between_vectors_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $x=(1,1)$ and $y=(-1,0)$, what type of angle is formed?","options":["Zero.","Obtuse.","Right.","Acute."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"302e0c2955987ab3c0da5af0"}]}}